message.content:
Sure! Below is a quick‑start guide so you can post your “Confirmed #malware” comment to **VirusTotal** against the IP `192.168.1.1`.  
Just copy/paste one of the snippets into your terminal (or adapt it for whatever scripting environment you prefer).

---

### 1️⃣ What you’ll need  

| Item | How to obtain |
|------|---------------|
| **API Key** | Your own key – e.g., `ABC321` (don’t share this publicly) |
| **Target ID** | You don’t actually need the internal numeric ID when posting a *new* comment—just reference the IP directly. |

---

### 2️⃣ Example using `curl`

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash

APIKEY="YOUR_API_KEY_HERE"
TARGET_IP="192.168.1.1"

cat <<EOF >payload.json
{
  "data": {
    "type": "comment",
    "attributes": {
      "text": "Confirmed #malware"
    },
    "relationships": {
      "targets": [
        {
          "type": "ipv4-address",
          "id": "$TARGET_IP"
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
EOF

curl --request POST \
     --url https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/comments \
     --header "accept: application/json" \
     --header "Content-Type: application/json" \
     --header "x-apikey: $APIKEY" \
     --data @payload.json
```

#### Quick notes

- Replace `"YOUR_API_KEY_HERE"` with your real key (`ABC321`).  
- The `relationships.targets[]` array tells VirusTotal which object the comment belongs to—in this case the IPv4 address itself.  
- If you’re working inside a script, just pipe the JSON straight into `--data`.

---

### 3️⃣ Optional: Using Python (`requests`)  

If you’d rather do it programmatically:

```python
import requests
import json

api_key   = "YOUR_API_KEY_HERE"
target_ip = "192.168.1.1"

headers = {
    "Accept":       "application/json",
    "Content-Type": "application/json",
    "x-apikey":     api_key,
}

body = {
    "data": {
        "type": "comment",
        "attributes": {
            "text": "Confirmed #malware"
        },
        "relationships": {
            "targets": [
                {
                    "type": "ipv4-address",
                    "id": target_ip
                }
            ]
        }
    }
}

resp = requests.post(
    url="https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/comments",
    headers=headers,
    data=json.dumps(body)
)

print(resp.status_code)
print(resp.text)
```

Again, swap out `YOUR_API_KEY_HERE` before running.

---

### 4️⃣ Verify the result

After a successful call you should receive something like:

```json
{
  "data": {
    "id": "<generated-comment-id>",
    "type": "comment",
    ...
  }
}
```

Open the VirusTotal web UI → *Comments* tab for the IP to see your entry appear.

---

That’s all! Once you’ve posted the comment, other analysts will be able to read it right away. Happy hunting 🚀
